'''Varakí TDV''' is a major railway interchange station in [[Varakí]], [[Estret Province]], [[Balboa]], situated on Avinguda Central in the city's Zona 3 central district. Opened in 2008 as part of the [[Sant Cristòfor–Areza HSR line|Sant Cristòfor–Areza high-speed rail line]], it is the most modern and largest station in the city, with eight tracks serving four island platforms. The station functions as the primary rail interchange for Varakí, connecting high-speed rail services on the [[Sant Cristòfor–Areza HSR line|Sant Cristòfor–Areza]] and [[Sant Cristòfor–Portnou HSR line|Sant Cristòfor–Portnou]] lines with [[CristoCarril]] [[Line C (CristoCarril)|Line C]] commuter services and [[Rodalies de Portnou]] Line 4 local services. It also houses a train yard serving both the high-speed rail fleet and CristoCarril rolling stock.

== History ==

Varakí TDV was constructed as part of the Sant Cristòfor–Areza high-speed rail project, which was completed in 2008 to provide a rapid rail link between the national capital and the International City of Areza. The station was built on a new alignment through central Varakí, elevated above the existing urban fabric on a viaduct that carries the high-speed tracks through the city. The site was selected for its position on Avinguda Central, the city's main boulevard, which provided a straight alignment and a central location accessible from all five municipal zones.

Prior to the construction of Varakí TDV, the only rail station in the city was [[Varakí Central (CristoCarril)|Varakí Central]], the original Zong-era station on the western edge of the city. Varakí Central's limited infrastructure — two platforms with passing tracks — constrained capacity for the growing number of commuter and intercity services using the Varakí Line corridor. The construction of Varakí TDV with eight tracks and four island platforms was intended to relieve this constraint and provide a modern interchange capable of handling high-speed rail, commuter rail, and local services simultaneously.

The station was designed and built by [[Intercon]], the national rail operator, as part of the HSR project. The train yard, situated adjacent to the station on the eastern side of the alignment, was included in the original construction to provide stabling and light maintenance for both the high-speed rail fleet and the CristoCarril commuter fleet assigned to Line C.

== Layout ==

[[File:Varaki HSR station layout.png|thumb|300px|Schematic layout of Varakí TDV, showing platform arrangement and track connections]]

Varakí TDV is an elevated station, with the platform level situated above the surrounding street level on a viaduct carrying the high-speed rail alignment through central Varakí. The station is oriented roughly north–south, following the alignment of Avinguda Central.

=== Platforms and tracks ===

The station has eight tracks serving four island platforms. The tracks are arranged in two groups:

* Tracks 1–2 (inner): High-speed rail through tracks, used by express HSR services that do not stop at Varakí. These tracks have no platform face and allow express trains to pass through the station at full speed without conflicting with stopping services.
* Tracks 3–4 (Platform A): HSR stopping tracks, used by high-speed rail services that serve Varakí. Platform A is the northernmost island platform.
* Tracks 5–6 (Platform B): CristoCarril Line C and Rodalies de Portnou Line 4 tracks. Platform B serves commuter and local services running on the Varakí Line corridor. Line C services terminate at and turn back from these tracks.
* Tracks 7–8 (Platforms C–D): Terminal tracks for CristoCarril Line C and Rodalies de Portnou Line 4. These tracks are used for dwell, turnaround, and turnaround manoeuvres for Line C services that have arrived from [[Varakí Central (CristoCarril)|Varakí Central]] and return to Varakí TDV for stabling or layover. Platform C and Platform D are the southernmost island platforms.

The arrangement allows simultaneous arrivals and departures of HSR, commuter, and local services without platform conflicts, a significant improvement over Varakí Central's two-platform layout.

=== Train yard ===

Adjacent to the station on the eastern side of the alignment, a train yard provides stabling and light maintenance facilities for both the high-speed rail fleet and the CristoCarril Line C commuter fleet. The yard consists of multiple sidings branching off the terminal tracks, with capacity for approximately 12 high-speed trainsets and 8 CristoCarril EMU sets. The yard includes a light maintenance shed with inspection pits, wheel-truing equipment, and cleaning facilities. Heavy maintenance for both fleets is carried out at Intercon's main workshops in Sant Cristòfor.

The yard's primary function is overnight stabling: high-speed trainsets that terminate their last service of the day at Areza are routed north to Varakí TDV for overnight stabling, reducing dead mileage to Sant Cristòfor. CristoCarril Line C sets that finish their evening service at Varakí Central are moved to the yard for overnight stabling and are dispatched from Varakí TDV the following morning.

=== Concourse and access ===

The station concourse is located at street level beneath the viaduct, with ticket halls, fare gates, and passenger information displays. Escalators, lifts, and stairs connect the concourse to the platform level above. The main entrance faces Avinguda Central, with secondary entrances on the side streets to the east and west. A covered drop-off area for buses and taxis is situated beneath the viaduct on the western side.

The station is connected to the [[Tramvia de Varakí]] streetcar Line C, which runs along Avinguda Central and links Varakí TDV to the [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Metro]] Line 9 corridor in the eastern neighbourhoods. The streetcar stop is located directly beneath the station concourse, providing a seamless interchange between the rail and streetcar systems.

== Services ==

=== High-speed rail ===

Varakí TDV is served by high-speed rail services on two lines operated by [[Intercon]]: the [[Sant Cristòfor–Areza HSR line|Sant Cristòfor–Areza HSR line]] and the [[Sant Cristòfor–Portnou HSR line|Sant Cristòfor–Portnou HSR line]]. Both lines share tracks between the [[Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal]] and Varakí TDV, diverging south of the station. Not all HSR services stop at Varakí; express services bypass the station on the inner through tracks (tracks 1–2) without stopping. Stopping services use Platform A (tracks 3–4). Journey time to Sant Cristòfor is approximately 12 minutes, to Areza approximately 8 minutes, and to Portnou approximately 15 minutes.

=== CristoCarril Line C ===

[[CristoCarril]] [[Line C (CristoCarril)|Line C]] serves Varakí TDV on Platform B (tracks 5–6). Line C services operate between the [[Sant Cristòfor Central Terminal]] and [[Varakí Central (CristoCarril)|Varakí Central]], with Varakí TDV as the penultimate southbound stop. After arriving at Varakí Central, the southern terminus, Line C services turn back northward to Varakí TDV, where they may dwell on the terminal tracks (tracks 7–8, Platforms C–D) before being dispatched for their next northbound working. This operating pattern uses Varakí TDV as the operational turnaround point for Line C, while Varakí Central serves as the passenger terminus.

=== Rodalies de Portnou Line 4 ===

[[Rodalies de Portnou]] Line 4 (RdP L4) serves Varakí TDV on Platform B (tracks 5–6), sharing the Varakí Line tracks with CristoCarril Line C between Varakí TDV and Varakí Central. RdP L4 provides local services continuing south from Varakí Central through the canal corridor to the [[Portnou]] metropolitan area. Varakí TDV serves as the northern terminal point for some RdP L4 services, with trains dwelling on the terminal tracks before being dispatched southward.

== Role in the network ==

Varakí TDV was designed to address the capacity constraints of [[Varakí Central (CristoCarril)|Varakí Central]], the city's older station. Varakí Central has only two platforms with passing tracks, a layout inherited from the original Zong-era railway. This configuration limits the number of trains that can stop or dwell simultaneously, creating scheduling conflicts when multiple services — Line C, RdP L4, and intercity services — converge on the station. The construction of Varakí TDV with eight tracks and four island platforms provided the additional capacity needed to handle the growing number of services on the Varakí Line corridor, particularly after the introduction of high-speed rail in 2008.

The station's role as the operational hub for Line C — with turnaround and dwell functions using the terminal tracks — reflects the constraints of Varakí Central's two-platform layout. By shifting the turnaround manoeuvre to Varakí TDV, Line C services can clear Varakí Central's platforms quickly, freeing them for RdP L4 and intercity services. The train yard further supports this role by providing stabling capacity adjacent to the turnaround tracks, minimising empty movements between the station and distant depots.

